WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.629
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.582
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:15.600
Unit five: Human Rights. Today you will use a preposition before which and whom in formal writing.

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.436
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.726
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:25.037
Universal rights.

7
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.814
Universal rights means rights that belong to every person everywhere.

8
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.949
Universal rights apply whatever a person's nationality.

9
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.934
Your turn. Say: Universal rights.

10
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.382
Universal rights.

11
00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:44.292
Discrimination.

12
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:49.245
Discrimination means unfair treatment of people because of who they are.

13
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.163
The law prohibits discrimination at work.

14
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.309
Your turn. Say: Discrimination.

15
00:00:58.167 --> 00:00:59.319
Discrimination.

16
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:01.247
Equality.

17
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.608
Equality means the state of being treated the same way as others.

18
00:01:06.217 --> 00:01:09.679
Equality before the law is a basic principle.

19
00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:12.854
Your turn. Say: Equality.

20
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:16.794
Equality.

21
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:18.760
Freedom of speech.

22
00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.178
Freedom of speech means the right to say what you think without punishment.

23
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:27.164
Freedom of speech allows citizens to criticise leaders.

24
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.600
Your turn. Say: Freedom of speech.

25
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:34.667
Freedom of speech.

26
00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:36.650
Justice system.

27
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.913
Justice system means the courts, police and laws that decide guilt and punishment.

28
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:45.515
A fair justice system treats everyone equally.

29
00:01:46.117 --> 00:01:48.815
Your turn. Say: Justice system.

30
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:53.028
Justice system.

31
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:54.947
Human dignity.

32
00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.481
Human dignity means the equal worth and respect every person deserves.

33
00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:03.211
Torture violates human dignity.

34
00:02:03.817 --> 00:02:06.597
Your turn. Say: Human dignity.

35
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:10.609
Human dignity.

36
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.734
International law.

37
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.839
International law means the rules that govern relations between countries.

38
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.645
International law forbids attacks on civilians.

39
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.344
Your turn. Say: International law.

40
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.607
International law.

41
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.398
Advocacy.

42
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:35.637
Advocacy means public support for a cause or group.

43
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.366
Advocacy helped to free the prisoner.

44
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.591
Your turn. Say: Advocacy.

45
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:45.551
Advocacy.

46
00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:49.983
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:50.817 --> 00:02:57.695
Visal, a charter says 'These are the rights to which every person is entitled.' Why not 'which every person is entitled to'?

48
00:02:58.783 --> 00:03:08.750
Both are correct. Putting the preposition before the relative pronoun is more formal: the rights to which, the system in which, the law under which.

49
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.304
Can we do that with 'who'?

50
00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:21.768
Only as 'whom': the people for whom the law was written. We cannot say 'for who' in formal writing, and we cannot use 'that' after a preposition.

51
00:03:22.883 --> 00:03:25.254
Give me an example about justice.

52
00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:37.629
A justice system in which everyone is treated equally protects human dignity. Discrimination is a practice against which international law offers protection.

53
00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.197
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:46.057
Grammar focus: Preposition plus relative pronoun: to which and for whom.

55
00:03:46.357 --> 00:03:47.567
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.923
These are the rights to which every person is entitled.

57
00:03:51.817 --> 00:03:55.198
It is a justice system in which everyone is treated equally.

58
00:03:56.083 --> 00:03:58.315
They are the people for whom the law was written.

59
00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:06.769
In formal writing, put the preposition before which / whom: the rights to which..., the people for whom... Never use 'that' or 'who' after a preposition.

60
00:04:07.517 --> 00:04:11.109
Be careful! Don't say: The people for who it was written.

61
00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:15.925
Say: The people for whom it was written.

62
00:04:16.225 --> 00:04:18.144
Use 'whom' after a preposition.

63
00:04:18.933 --> 00:04:21.377
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.167 --> 00:04:23.922
Rights That Belong to Everyone.

65
00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:29.600
Human rights are not gifts from governments; they are the standards to which every state should be held.

66
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:38.254
Universal rights, such as freedom of speech and equality before the law, apply to everyone, whatever their nationality.

67
00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:45.423
Discrimination is a practice against which international law offers protection, yet enforcement remains uneven.

68
00:04:46.433 --> 00:04:51.800
A fair justice system is one in which a person's dignity is respected at every stage.

69
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.505
Advocacy groups are the organisations through which ordinary people can make these promises real.

70
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.555
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.417 --> 00:05:06.182
These are the rights to blank everyone is entitled.

72
00:05:06.482 --> 00:05:09.509
A, that. B, which. C, what.

73
00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:14.915
The answer is B: which.

74
00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.094
Treating people unfairly because of who they are is blank.

75
00:05:19.394 --> 00:05:23.663
A, discrimination. B, advocacy. C, sovereignty.

76
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.513
The answer is A: discrimination.

77
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.371
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:32.671 --> 00:05:38.832
A, The people for who it was written.. B, The people for whom it was written.. C, The people for that it was written..

79
00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:45.283
The answer is B: The people for whom it was written..

80
00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.007
Rules between countries are called blank law.

81
00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:53.239
A, local. B, personal. C, international.

82
00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.765
The answer is C: international.

83
00:05:59.917 --> 00:06:04.215
According to the reading, advocacy groups help people make promises…

84
00:06:04.515 --> 00:06:07.959
A, real. B, secret. C, shorter.

85
00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.163
The answer is A: real.

86
00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:17.402
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:22.014
Why are human rights considered universal, regardless of country?

88
00:06:30.817 --> 00:06:32.294
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.594 --> 00:06:57.296
Human rights are called universal because they come from the equal dignity of every person, not from a government's decision. A person does not lose these rights by crossing a border or living under a different system. International law reflects this idea, which is why states are expected to protect rights such as equality and freedom of speech for everyone.

90
00:06:57.596 --> 00:06:59.282
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:00.183 --> 00:07:06.713
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and preposition plus relative pronoun: to which and for whom.

92
00:07:07.013 --> 00:07:11.999
Today's value is human dignity. Every person deserves respect wherever they are.

93
00:07:12.299 --> 00:07:14.801
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:17.800
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

